  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Prepositioning Shelter Support:  Strengthening Local Resilience through Improved Disaster Preparedness in Typhoon-Prone, Vulnerable Communities in the Philippines</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Philippines is one of the most high-risk countries in the world in terms of frequency and intensity of natural hazards. Approximately 74 per cent of the population and 60 per cent of the land area is exposed to various climate risks. In addition to 20 typhoons each year, this includes major tsunamis, earthquakes, volcanic eruptions, floods and landslides, resulting in frequent and complex emergency settings and situations of displacement. The project seeks to improve the ability of local governments, especially Department of Social Welfare and Development (DSWD) and Department of Human Settlements and Urban Development (DHSUD), to coordinate and timely address the needs of vulnerable population affected by disasters. IOM endeavours to reduce the vulnerabilities of the communities to typhoons by providing them with access to typhoon resilient shelters and improving their collective awareness on disaster preparedness and management through simulation exercises and trainings. It also augments the governments&#x2019; efforts in prepositioning of relief items and preparing of emergency teams to deliver timely and efficient shelter response services.</narrative>
  </description>
